The spec is written. Here's what it covers:

**`requirements.md`** — 13 EARS-format requirements spanning: git log ingestion with configurable date ranges, file filtering (binaries, lockfiles, vendored dirs), deleted-block extraction with configurable minimum run length, move detection via exact normalized-text matching across the same commit, normalization (whitespace, comments, optional identifier renaming), k-gram winnowing fingerprinting (FNV-1a, configurable k and window), tombstone construction with all fields, language detection from extensions, link extraction from `#123` and GitHub URLs, deterministic JSON serialization, the full CLI interface with flags, a 60-second performance target, and the browser-safety module boundary.

**`design.md`** — covers the full data-flow pipeline (stream → parse → extract → move-filter → fingerprint → accumulate → serialize), all key TypeScript types, per-module design decisions (sentinel-based commit boundary detection, hand-rolled skip-list without a glob library, inline FNV-1a, two-pointer Jaccard, SHA-1 for stable ids), streaming architecture to stay under ~50 MB memory, determinism guarantees for each source of non-determinism, and an error-handling table.

**`tasks.md`** — 16 ordered tasks:
1. Core types
2. `normalize.ts` + 4 fast-check property tests (idempotency, whitespace invariance, comment-only invariance, renameIds symmetry)
3. `fingerprint.ts` + 6 property tests (determinism, self-similarity, symmetry, bounded, short-text empty, sorted)
4. `match.ts` + 5 property tests (identity, symmetry, bounded, monotonicity, empty)
5–6. Language detection and link parser (unit tests)
7–9. Diff parser, block extractor, move filter (unit tests + 3 property tests)
10–11. Git log streamer and tombstone builder with fixture-repo tests
12–13. Orchestrator with integration tests and sorted JSON serializer
14. CLI `index` command
15. Coverage audit and `research/pbt-findings.md`
16. Performance smoke script against a real public repo
